# Overview
#
# A vague audience is a pitch to every possible reader (`Whether you're
# a beginner or an expert, the CLI fits your workflow`). The pitch does
# not name a particular reader, so no reader learns whether the feature
# suits them. The fix names who the feature is for or drops the pitch.
# The rule sits at Recommended, because a real condition can open with
# the same words.
#
# Expression
#
# `you` holds `you're` and `you are`. The `or` branches match `whether`,
# a form from `you`, `a` or `an`, and then `or` after one, two, or three
# words. A list picks its longest match and never backtracks, so each
# gap length is its own alternative. The `new to` branch matches
# `whether you're new to` without the `or`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `whether you are a` also opens a real condition (`the check reports
# whether you are a member of the group`), so the pitch needs the `or`
# that pairs two kinds of reader. `whether you're new to` counts without
# the `or`, because a condition rarely asks it. A condition that does
# ask it still draws a finding (`the form asks whether you are new to
# the team`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule did not hit any line in the measure.py corpus, so no sample
# measures its precision.
expr you [you're, (you are)]
expr main [(whether @you [a, an] * or), (whether @you [a, an] * * or), (whether @you [a, an] * * * or), (whether @you new to)]
let message "Vague audience. Name who the feature is for, or delete the clause."
let description "Flags a clause that pitches to every kind of reader, such as `whether you're a beginner or an expert`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The check reports whether you are a member of the group."
